- Story & Context: Legend holds that during a freak fogbank in late 1998, the band navigated their sound-barge through a submerged, unmapped 19th-century canal system that didn't appear on any municipal blueprints. As the boat glided between forgotten stone archways, the glass slide on the bass guitar resonated in harmony with the ancient underwater masonry, creating a standing wave that temporarily cleared the fog for three miles. "We are floating over streets that forgot their own names," whispered the late market elder Mae Thip into the delay pedal as the boat drifted back into the main river at sunrise. The track ends with four minutes of isolated, dry bass-slaps echoing against unseen brick structures, anchored only by the faint, distant hiss of a 90s cassette tape running out of magnetic lead.
